                "type": "text",
                "text": "Equate the two sums to find the smallest integer, then add 6.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Let $n$ be the smallest integer. The smallest seven are $n,n+1,\\ldots,n+6$, with sum $7n+21$. The largest six are $n+7,n+8,\\ldots,n+12$, with sum $6n+57$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}7n+21&=6n+57\\\\n+21&=57\\\\n&=36\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe middle integer is the seventh integer:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}n+6&=36+6\\\\n+6&=42\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
